 ## Overview
-Compression reduces the memory footprint and transmission bandwidth of volumetric datasets by exploiting spatial redundancy, value correlation, or structural sparsity, enabling the rendering of large-scale or time-varying volumes that would otherwise exceed hardware limits [S1, unofficial][S5]. This family encompasses methods ranging from lossless entropy coding and sparse data structures to lossy quantization, dictionary learning, and neural representations [S1, unofficial][S2][S5]. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [Dense grids and memory layout](3-dense-grids-and-memory-layout.md) (which focuses on access patterns) or [Multiresolution and level of detail](7-multiresolution-and-level-of-detail.md) (which focuses on geometric approximation) is the explicit transformation of data values or topology to reduce bit-count, often at the cost of reconstruction fidelity or increased decoding complexity [S1, unofficial][S5].
+Compression reduces the memory footprint and transmission bandwidth of volumetric datasets by exploiting spatial redundancy, value correlation, or structural sparsity, enabling the rendering of large-scale or time-varying volumes that would otherwise exceed hardware limits [S1, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. This family encompasses methods ranging from lossless entropy coding and sparse data structures to lossy quantization, dictionary learning, and neural representations [S1, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [Dense grids and memory layout](3-dense-grids-and-memory-layout.md) (which focuses on access patterns) or [Multiresolution and level of detail](7-multiresolution-and-level-of-detail.md) (which focuses on geometric approximation) is the explicit transformation of data values or topology to reduce bit-count, often at the cost of reconstruction fidelity or increased decoding complexity [S1, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All compression methods in this family share the following mechanism:
 
-1.  **Redundancy Exploitation:** The core principle is to identify and eliminate redundancy in the volumetric data. This redundancy can be spatial (empty space, homogeneous regions), statistical (correlated values between neighbors), or structural (repetitive patterns in time-varying data) [S1, unofficial][S5].
-2.  **Transformation/Encoding:** Data is transformed into a domain where redundancy is more apparent or compact. This includes spatial subdivision (sparse structures), frequency domain transformation (wavelets/pyramids), or dictionary-based representation (sparse coding, vector quantization) [S1, unofficial][S2][S5].
-3.  **Quantization (Lossy):** For lossy compression, continuous or high-precision values are mapped to a discrete set of representatives. This step is critical for achieving high compression ratios but introduces reconstruction error, which must be managed via metrics like PSNR or perceptual quality [S1, unofficial][S2][S5].
-4.  **Entropy Coding:** The transformed or quantized data is encoded into a bitstream using entropy coding techniques (e.g., Huffman, Arithmetic, rANS) to approach the Shannon entropy limit of the source [S1, unofficial][S5].
-5.  **Decompression/Reconstruction:** Rendering requires decoding the bitstream and reconstructing the volume data, either fully or partially (e.g., via LOD or adaptive loading), to provide input to the ray-marching or ray-casting pipeline [S1, unofficial][S5].
