 ### [Sparse Coding (K-SVD)](1018-sparse-coding-k-svd.md)
-Sparse Coding (K-SVD) is a data-driven compression technique that represents volume-rendered images or volumetric data blocks as sparse linear combinations of atoms from an overcomplete dictionary learned via the K-SVD algorithm. It solves the problem of high storage and transmission costs for large volume datasets by achieving high compression ratios (often under 1 bit per sample) while maintaining visual fidelity. Unlike prescribed orthogonal bases like DCT, K-SVD learns a dictionary tailored to the specific statistical properties of the volume rendering data, offering superior reconstruction quality for missing pixel recovery and compression artifacts. It differs from Vector Quantization (VQ) by allowing linear combinations of multiple dictionary atoms rather than selecting a single nearest neighbor, providing higher fidelity at the cost of more complex encoding.
+Sparse Coding (K-SVD) compresses volume rendering data by learning an overcomplete dictionary from rendered images and representing image blocks as sparse linear combinations of dictionary atoms. It sets itself apart from prescribed basis methods like [Wavelet and Pyramid Transforms](3856-wavelet-and-pyramid-transforms.md) by adapting the basis to the specific statistical structure of the volume data, and from [Vector Quantization for Volumetric Radiance Fields](1870-vector-quantization-for-volumetric-radiance-fields.md) by allowing multiple atoms per block, which reduces reconstruction error at the cost of higher encoding complexity.
 
-### [16-bit Floating Point Format for Spectra](1163-16-bit-floating-point-format-for-spectra.md)
-A custom 16-bit floating point format used to represent the frequency domain spectrum, reducing memory cost compared to 64-bit double precision while maintaining acceptable precision. This method is specific to spectral volume rendering applications where high dynamic range is required but full double precision is unnecessary. It sets itself apart by targeting the specific numerical requirements of spectral data rather than general volumetric density or color.
+### [S3Dc GPU Decompression and Rendering](1190-s3dc-gpu-decompression-and-rendering.md)
+S3Dc (Scalar 3Dc) is a lossy volume compression algorithm for scalar data that enables direct GPU rendering by storing compressed data in two 3D textures: a MinMax Volume (MMV) and an Index Volume (IV). Unlike standard texture compression formats like DXT or VTC which are designed for RGB(A) color data and cause block artifacts on non-smooth scalar fields, S3Dc is tailored for scalar volumetric datasets, allowing the GPU to reconstruct voxel values on-the-fly during rendering without storing decompressed data in memory. This approach reduces CPU-GPU transfer bottlenecks and allows interactive frame rates for large volumes that would otherwise exceed GPU memory limits.
 
 ### [PlenOctree Compression](1341-plenoctree-compression.md)
 PlenOctree Compression is a file-size reduction strategy designed to make PlenOctrees feasible for in-browser rendering by minimizing occupied voxels and applying direct compression to the tree structure. It distinguishes itself from general value compression [6-value-compression.md] by specifically targeting the spherical harmonic (SH) coefficients of PlenOctrees using median-cut quantization and DEFLATE encoding, and from sparse structures [5-sparse-structures.md] by compressing the entire tree including pointers rather than just the data values.
 
-### [Volume Normalization](1823-volume-normalization.md)
-Volume Normalization is a preprocessing method that scales a volume tensor to the range $[0, 1]$ while ensuring that constant positive masks are preserved as full foreground ($1.0$). It differs from general value compression by focusing on range mapping rather than bit-depth reduction, and from sparse structures by operating on dense data layouts. It is often a prerequisite for effective quantization or transfer function application.
-
-### [Vector Quantization for Volumetric Radiance Fields](1870-vector-quantization-for-volumetric-radiance-fields.md)
-Vector Quantization (VQ) is a dictionary-based compression strategy that encodes voxel color features into a compact codebook, substituting multiple voxel features with a single code vector to reduce storage from individual voxel storage to codebook storage plus mapping indices. It solves the problem of high memory consumption for color features in volumetric radiance fields by enabling per-voxel random access through on-the-fly reconstruction. Unlike sparse structures that store explicit coordinates or value compression that quantizes individual values, VQ learns a shared representation space. It sets itself apart by allowing a trade-off between compression ratio and rendering quality via a "keep quantile" that preserves the most important voxels uncompressed.
-
 ### [Arithmetic Encoding](2127-arithmetic-encoding.md)
-Arithmetic encoding is a lossless entropy coding technique that maps a sequence of input symbols to a single fractional number within the interval $[0, 1)$. It solves the inefficiency of fixed-length or symbol-by-symbol variable-length codes by encoding the entire message as one number, achieving a bit rate approaching the Shannon entropy limit. It differs from [Run-Length Encoding for Voxel Data](5051-run-length-encoding-for-voxel-data.md) by not relying on explicit run-length prefixes, and from [rANS (Asymmetric Numeral Systems)](5011-rans-asymmetric-numeral-systems.md) by using floating-point interval arithmetic rather than integer state machines, though rANS is often preferred for hardware parallelism.
-
-### [Volume Utility Functions](2677-volume-utility-functions.md)
-Volume Utility Functions are a collection of low-level helper routines used to prepare, validate, and transform volumetric data for compression and rendering pipelines. They solve problems related to tensor dimensionality alignment, physical unit normalization (Hounsfield units), spatial resampling, and memory layout corrections. Unlike [Sparse structures](5-sparse-structures.md) which organize data topology, or [Value compression](6-value-compression.md) which reduces bit-depth, these utilities ensure data integrity and format compatibility before compression or rendering occurs. They serve as the foundational preprocessing and postprocessing steps for the [Compression](4-compression.md) pipeline.
+Arithmetic encoding is a lossless entropy coding technique that maps a sequence of input symbols to a single fractional number within the interval $[0, 1)$ [G]. It solves the inefficiency of fixed-length or symbol-by-symbol variable-length codes by encoding the entire message as one number, achieving a bit rate approaching the Shannon entropy limit [G]. In volume rendering, it is used to compress anchor attributes and other metadata in neural representations by encoding them into a binary bitstream based on probabilities estimated by a context model.
 
 ### [Feature-Preserving Volume Data Reduction and Focus+Context Visualization](2983-feature-preserving-volume-data-reduction-and-focus-context-v.md)
-This method reduces volumetric data size while preserving features of interest and enabling focus+context visualization by deforming the 3D grid space to magnify important regions and compress unimportant ones, followed by resampling. Unlike uniform downsampling which discards information oblivious to data content, or multiresolution methods that require extra data packing or blending to handle cross-resolution boundaries, this method uses continuous deformation to intelligently allocate more samples to important features and sparsely sample unimportant regions, guaranteeing similar resolutions along neighboring grid boundaries and avoiding seams.
