 ## Background
-Compression for volume rendering has evolved from simple lossless techniques like Run-Length Encoding (RLE) and Huffman coding to sophisticated lossy methods involving wavelet transforms, vector quantization, and deep learning. Early work focused on reducing the massive memory requirements of dense grids for direct volume rendering (DVR) [S1, unofficial][S5]. The introduction of wavelet and pyramid transforms allowed for multi-resolution access and adaptive rendering [S1, unofficial][S2][S5]. More recently, with the rise of neural radiance fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting, compression has shifted towards dictionary learning, vector quantization, and structural pruning to handle the high-dimensional feature spaces of these representations [S1, S6, unofficial]. Sparse structures have also seen renewed interest for handling large-scale outdoor or medical volumes with significant empty space [S1, S4, unofficial].
